content.text:
{
  "primary_meaning": "Distant Gentile nations or remote coastlands of the earth that will receive God's law and salvation",
  "primary_basis": [
    "Genesis 10:5",
    "Isaiah 42:4",
    "Isaiah 42:10",
    "Isaiah 49:1",
    "Isaiah 51:5",
    "Isaiah 60:9",
    "Psalm 72:10",
    "Psalm 97:1"
  ],
  "alternatives": [
    {
      "meaning": "Sites of isolation, exile, or separation (literal or spiritual)",
      "basis": [
        "Revelation 1:9 (Patmos)",
        "geographic isolation motif in prophetic and apostolic settings"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Elements of creation that flee or vanish under divine judgment",
      "basis": [
        "Revelation 6:14",
        "Revelation 16:20"
      ],
      "prominence": "common"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "Arenas of missionary advance or temporary refuge for the gospel",
      "basis": [
        "Acts 13 (Cyprus)",
        "Acts 27–28 (Malta)",
        "general NT island itineraries"
      ],
      "prominence": "minority"
    },
    {
      "meaning": "The church as solid ground amid the chaotic sea of the world",
      "basis": [
        "allegorical extensions of sea = nations/chaos imagery"
      ],
      "prominence": "rare"
    }
  ],
  "has_settled_consensus": true,
  "confidence": "medium",
  "notes": "The Hebrew ʾiyyîm (often rendered “isles”/“islands”/“coastlands”) is treated in standard Christian biblical interpretation primarily as a prophetic metonym for far-off Gentile peoples rather than a freestanding typological symbol. Literal geographic references and eschatological instability motifs are secondary but well-attested; highly allegorical church-as-island readings remain marginal."
}
